nodecode-jev-skill-router is a rewrite of ydmw74's ydmw74/jev-skill-router:
https://github.com/ydmw74/jev-skill-router
commit 76ee09e049dbc10c76f98cfefc6ca16b42cfad38

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 ydmw74 (LICENSE)

The repository ships no LICENSE file; plugin.json declares "license": "MIT"
and its author's URL is the GitHub handle above, which is the holder used here.

Rewritten: the whole selection the tool made -- the one `choice` over the live
roster on the index-shortened descriptions, the three request judgments
(`acts_on_user_system`, `would_follow_documented_procedure`, and the inverted
`prose_suffices`) and their mean as the gate, the second call's rerank of the
shortlist with each candidate's full description and 700 characters of its own
text plus one `fits` judgment each, both thresholds (0.30/0.30), the
`none_of_these` option, the shortlist of three, the answer object
(`decision`, `suggested`, `gate`, `gate_values`, `shortlist`, `fits`,
`suggestion_block`, usage, seconds), the two backends (a TypeSafe key, or an
adapter endpoint with its own bearer token) and the fail-safe posture: a call
with no credential names what to set and spends nothing, and a failure is a
field in the answer rather than an exception. The roster, read in the original
from `$HERMES_HOME/skills` and their `SKILL.md` front matter, is here what an
index lists -- the skills this image keeps. The MCP tool is Nodecode's
`skill_select`; the operator reaches the same decision through
`/jev-skill-router`.

Left out: the stdio MCP server loop, its JSON-RPC framing and its `uv`
launcher, because Nodecode dispatches a declared tool itself; and the
per-session roster cache, because a live index is cheap to read and a stale
roster is what the original's own comment warns about. The companion
`SKILL.md` is carried as `(help :jev-skill-router)`. Python's stdlib client
became a hand-written JSON writer and `nlk:http`.
